format session-log-v1
stamp 20260905.090338
editor Claude Code CLI
provider Anthropic
product Claude Code
role Petrichor -- docs-geode and the prose product, tree grain-petrichor
modality text
model claude-opus-5
model_status active -- the harness system prompt declares the running model by exact id
configured_model claude-opus-5
configured_reasoning max
configured_status .claude/settings.json is tracked and proves the clone default, never the running session
voice Kyri
host NixOS 6.18.49 x86_64 AMD pier (Vultr Dallas), one clock America/New_York
title The stash that held a finished lap
prompt The baton, unattended lap 3939. Rota row 4 by 3939 mod 5 -- Earth, the row that breathes in.
think The round open printed stashed_entries=1 before it printed anything else, and the card's own %321 clause says to open there. The stash held fourteen files, a session log stamped 20260905.083758, and a REDS row -- a complete lap that died at the commit boundary six minutes before I read the clock. The Earth row asks for the concrete fact at the door, and the concrete fact was that a finished repair was sitting in a place git does not show anyone by default.
think My first question was whether it was mine to land. One writer per checkout, and this is grain-petrichor with no peer in flight, so the stash was the previous Petrichor lap's own leavings. Landing it is finishing a lap rather than adopting a peer's work.
think My second question was whether it was RIGHT, and I refused to take the elder log's word for it. Fetch-before-book put xy/main at 8b8fc414ff, equal to local HEAD; reds_spine_derive_scan --next answered 418, which is what the row books. All four repaired index rows resolve to logs that exist. The elder version of the guard ran green in my cold pass at 1s, which is the fault stated as a measurement -- it passes because the room it watches is empty by law.
think Then I ran the stashed scan from a copy in /tmp rather than applying it, so the tree stayed still under a cold roster pass I had already started. It found exactly four post-law gaps, two count drifts, and one missing pin row, and refused with verdict=shelf_row_missing. The repair does what its row claims.
obs I nearly corrected something that was not wrong. The scan gates every day shelf carries a pin row, and I measured 72 day directories on disk against 44 index files and read that as a 30-shelf overstatement. Reading the pin's own tail showed why it is not: the elder days are covered by two aggregate shelves, README-index-through-20260721.md at 775 rows and README-index-20260722-shelf.md at 80, and the pin names both. The word shelf in this tree means the index page rather than the directory, and the code was right. Measurement before edit is what stopped that, and it is worth writing down because the edit would have read as a careful fix.
obs The same measurement then found a real one, three lines from where the control disproves it. The scan, the witness header and the REDS row each said the 20260712-20260717 block is one no index in this tree reaches, and behavior 19 of that lap's own control reads a README-index-through-YYYYMMDD gathering for its links and asserts gathering_links_counted=yes. Counted against the population the guard itself reads, the aggregate shelf carries 162 of that block's 373 logs and 211 stand unwritten, and 162 plus 211 is 373 exactly. The sentence is true of DAY-named shelves and false of shelves generally. The number 211 was right the whole time; only the claim around it outran the mechanism.
obs That is %418's own subject arriving in %418's own prose -- a guard's claim outrunning what it measures -- and the honest reading is that the elder lap checked for README-index-20260712.md through README-index-20260717.md, found none, and drew a conclusion true of everything it looked at. I corrected the three living sites and left the elder log exactly as written, because a dated log is testimony and its author's belief that morning is the thing it records.
obs Grading the front door produced the sharpest finding of the lap. session-logs/README.md reads A/93 at truth_counted=100, and on the tree I opened it, its top row named 20260904 open while seven laps stood on the 20260905 shelf. Every one of its 54 cited paths resolved. That is %117's shape and %417's shape a third time in one day, and the counted half of Truth cannot see any of the three.
obs What separates this firing from the two before it is that its claim is derivable. The newest shelf and a closed day's count are both computable from the room, so this lap gates them at zero rather than leaving them to a reader who may not come. %117's claim about a fresh clone and %417's about a fused pointer are not derivable, and they stay in the judged half. So the lantern-becomes-a-loom law resolves here for one class of claim and honestly does not for the other.
obs I held the tree still for the whole cold pass rather than editing under it, which is what the elder lap did not manage and named in its own log. 111 guards, 1,101s, 104 green, tree_moved=no. The two reds are fleet_roster at three control failures and agent_jail, both already standing on the card as this pier's named pair, and neither is the prose lane.
obs Then xy moved under me. A second fetch before the send found two commits landed, and both halves of my round had collided: a peer booked %418 at stamp 20260905.081211 while I was verifying, and folded the same three rows 414 to 416 onto a shelf of its own name. The derived-spine law decides this without argument -- the key is the stamp, a published number never moves, and renumbering is confined to unshared rows. 081211 reached the anointed spine first, so it keeps 418 and my row became 419 carrying its own stamp unchanged. I dropped my duplicate fold shelf and adopted the peer's, since two archives of one fold is worse than either.
obs Reading the ledger line by line to place the renumbered row is what found the second red. A grep for the census headline returned two hits where one was wanted, and md5sum on each line proved them byte-identical. It entered with 60776bcad and rode through e8e62349f unremarked. reds_ledger_monotone reads GREEN over it, measured on metal before I wrote the row: its duplicate_headlines reading counts ROW headlines sharing one number, which is a different question from how many census headlines a page carries, and the headline check reads the first match and validates its three numbers against the spine. A guard can check what a line says and never how many times the page says it. Booked %420, line removed, census re-derived to 420 and 400 from the spine rather than incremented.
obs I named the cardinality assert rather than building it. A lantern earns its loom on the second firing and this is the first, so the honest record is the row plus the named gap, and the next lap that opens that witness pays one assert.
obs The card was at its ceiling and my two rows did not fit, so I folded rather than raised. The %412 and %413 accounts condensed into one paragraph holding every number, and the %411 and %410 accounts became one pointer at the shelves that already hold both rows whole. A checkpoint cairn went in first naming what left and why.
obs And the fold bit me, which is the part worth writing down. I took the span from %411's opening to %410's close as though the two were adjacent, and they were not -- the aroma breach and the card-bound paragraphs sat between them and went with the cut. Reading the diff rather than trusting the byte count found it: three greps, two of them zero. A span is not a section, and the only thing that told them apart was looking.
loom roster guards=111 wall_s=1101 green=104 red=2 gated=5 tree_moved=no
loom scan log_has_a_row logs=4056 shelves=44 post_law_gaps=4 pin_drift=2 pin_shelf_missing=1
loom block 20260712_20260717 logs=373 named_by_gathering=162 unwritten=211
loom qa session-logs/README.md composite=93 letter=A register=80 truth_counted=100
file tools/fixtures/l/log_has_a_row_scan.sh the ratchet comment now says reached in part, with the gathering shelf named and 162 of 373 counted
file tools/l/log_has_a_row_witness.rish the same correction in the header that a reader meets first
file construction/REDS.md %419 renumbered off the collision with its stamp intact, and %420 booked for the doubled census line
file construction/ITINERARY.md %419 and %420 seated, the nib at HEAD's parent, %412/%413 condensed and %411/%410 folded to a pointer
file construction/CHECKPOINTS.md the cairn for that fold, naming both readings so neither is lost
file session-logs/date/README-index-20260905.md this lap's row above the elder lap's
recommend keep-going -- the cardinality assert %420 names is one line on the next lap that opens reds_ledger_monotone, and the 211 unwritten rows in the 20260712-20260717 block are now a named and correctly described ceiling, and writing them from each log's own title field is a bounded lap whenever the queue reaches it
